\newlabel{fig:arch}{{8}{8}{Simulator architecture. The generator builds scenario inputs and hidden draws once, offline. The simulator reveals a request only when it arrives and applies a hidden draw only when its event happens. At each decision step the dispatcher (route generation, then the MILP) and the transfer rule see only the observable state; vans then drive and commit to their next stop}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\newlabel{fig:arch@cref}{{[figure][8][]8}{[1][6][]8}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{tab:example}{{7}{8}{Worked example arithmetic (minutes; both routes 10 km; dwell, the time stopped at the curb for boarding, omitted). $\varphi (12)=27.5$, $\varphi (8)=12.5$, $\varphi (16)=52.5$, $\varphi (6)=7.5$}{table.caption.18}{}}
\newlabel{tab:example@cref}{{[table][7][]7}{[1][8][]8}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:lifecycle}{{9}{9}{The life of one request. Left: the van path. Right: the transfer path. Red boxes are terminal outcomes; the dashed box (\texttt {lyft\_failed}) is used only in a sensitivity case where a Lyft order can fail. $\ell $ is the ordering lag (2 minutes in the pilot) and $\lambda _r$ the rider's hidden Lyft delay; \texttt {lyft\_booked} means the Lyft has been ordered. ``Deadline passes'' is the hidden patience deadline. A rider can cancel while still waiting for a van and while a van is driving to the curb. During the 3-minute wait for an absent rider, the dispatcher plans as if the rider might still board; the simulator never boards an absent rider}{figure.caption.15}{}}
\newlabel{fig:lifecycle@cref}{{[figure][9][]9}{[1][6][]9}{}{}{}}
